TITLE:: RLPulse
summary:: A pulse: the relation between time points a whole number of periods apart
categories:: Libraries>reLation
related:: Classes/RLTimePoints, Classes/RLTimeMap, Classes/RLMetre, Classes/RLNetwork, Guides/reLation

DESCRIPTION::
Popoff and Yust's metrical relation M(d): two time points are related when the time between them is a whole multiple of d. It holds between the points of every pulse layer of period d, wherever the layer starts. M(0) relates a point to itself alone: the identity.

Pulses compose to their greatest common divisor, meet in their least common multiple, and one is included in another when its period is a whole multiple of the other's. Both the composition and the inclusion mean something: a metre is a chain of pulses each included in the next (the bar, the beat, the division of the beat), and two metres are at odds when their chains part (a grouping dissonance: the hemiola).

Periods are plain numbers read as fractions of whole numbers (code::1/6::, code::0.25::, code::3::): the nearest one with a denominator up to 10000 (a period under a ten-thousandth keeps a larger one), so a period that is no such fraction is replaced by the nearest. Two time points count as a whole number of periods apart when what is left over is under a billionth of the time between them (or of 1). A pulse is a value. link::Classes/RLTimePoints:: is the context where pulses label networks; link::Classes/RLMetre:: has the same relations on a cycle of n pulses, as finite relations.

code::
RLPulse(1/2) <> RLPulse(1/3);          // RLPulse(1/6): the pulse both fit in
RLPulse(1/2) & RLPulse(1/3);           // RLPulse(1): the pulse that fits in both
RLPulse(1) <= RLPulse(1/2);            // true
RLPulse(1/2).relates(1/2, 3);          // true: five halves apart
RLPulse(1/4).points(0, 1, 1/12);       // [1/12, 1/3, 7/12, 5/6]

// Cohn's ski-hill graph of a bar of six quarters down to eighths (Brahms, violin sonata op. 78)
RLPulse.paths(6, 1/2);                 // [6, 3, 1.5, 0.5], [6, 3, 1, 0.5], [6, 2, 1, 0.5]
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: period
A number, zero or more (its sign is dropped). 0 is the identity.

METHOD:: gcd
METHOD:: lcm
The longest period that fits a whole number of times in two periods (code::gcd(0, x):: is x), and the shortest that both fit in (code::lcm(0, x):: is 0).

METHOD:: fraction
A period as code::[numerator, denominator]::.

METHOD:: skiHill
Cohn's ski-hill graph between two periods, the top a whole multiple of the bottom: the periods that divide the top and that the bottom divides, the longest first.

METHOD:: paths
The paths of that graph: each a list of periods from top to bottom, every step a division by a prime number (2 and 3 in the usual metres). A path is a metre: code::[6, 3, 1]:: is six quarters in two beats of three, code::[6, 2, 1]:: in three beats of two.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: period

METHOD:: relates
Whether two time points are a whole number of periods apart.

METHOD:: <>
This after that: their greatest common divisor.

METHOD:: &
What two pulses both hold: their least common multiple.

METHOD:: <=
Included in that: every two points this relates, that relates. This period is a whole multiple of that one's; the identity is included in every pulse.

METHOD:: scaled
The pulse of a time stretched by a.

METHOD:: points
The time points of the layer of this period through code::phase::, from code::from:: up to code::to:: (itself left out).

METHOD:: name
M and the period as a fraction: code::"M1/6"::.

METHOD:: isIdentity
METHOD:: label
